==================================================================================================
1. FUNDAMENTALS
==================================================================================================

Q1.1 ⭐ What is a Version Control System (VCS), and why is it important?
- A system that records changes to files over time so you can recall any version later.
- Why it matters: full history (who, what, when, why), safe collaboration, branching for parallel work,
  rollback when something breaks, auditability, and it is the base for CI/CD.

Q1.2 ⭐ What is the difference between Centralized and Distributed VCS?
- Centralized (SVN, Perforce): one central server holds the history. Clients check out a working copy.
  Most operations need the network, and the server is a single point of failure.
- Distributed (Git, Mercurial): every clone is a full copy of the repository, including all history.
  Commit, log, diff and branch are local and fast. You can work offline, and every clone is a backup.

Q1.3 What is SVN?
- Apache Subversion, a centralized VCS. It uses revision numbers (r1, r2...) instead of hashes, and
  branches are directory copies. Commits go straight to the server.
- Compared with Git: branching and merging are heavier, and most commands need the network.

Q1.4 ⭐ What is the difference between Git and GitHub?
- Git is the version control tool. It is open source, runs locally, and was created by Linus Torvalds in 2005.
- GitHub is a cloud hosting platform for Git repositories. On top of Git it adds pull requests, code
  review, issues, Actions (CI/CD), branch protection, security scanning and project boards.
- Alternatives to GitHub: GitLab, Bitbucket, Azure Repos.

Q1.5 What are the advantages of using Git?
- Distributed (offline work, every clone is a backup).
- Fast, because most operations are local.
- Cheap, lightweight branching.
- Data integrity: every object is addressed by its hash, so corruption is detected.
- Staging area, which lets you build commits carefully.
- Huge ecosystem (GitHub/GitLab, CI tools, IDE support).

Q1.6 What is a repository in Git?
- A project folder tracked by Git. It is the working files plus the .git directory, which holds all
  commits, branches, tags and config.

Q1.7 What are the different types of Git repositories?
- Non-bare (normal) repo: has a working tree plus .git. This is what you develop in.
- Bare repo (`git init --bare`): only the Git database, with no working tree. Used on servers and as a
  shared central repo, because you push to it and nobody edits files in it directly.
- By location: local repo (your machine) vs remote repo (GitHub or a server).

Q1.8 ⭐ What are the three main states a file can be in within Git?
- Modified: changed in the working directory, not yet staged.
- Staged: marked (with `git add`) to go into the next commit.
- Committed: safely stored in the local repository.
- (Also: untracked, which means Git has never seen the file, and ignored.)

Q1.9 ⭐ Explain the Git Lifecycle / Git Workflow.
  Working Directory --git add--> Staging Area (Index) --git commit--> Local Repo --git push--> Remote Repo
  Remote Repo --git fetch--> Local Repo (remote-tracking branches) --git merge/rebase--> your branch
  (`git pull` = fetch + merge, or fetch + rebase)
- File lifecycle: Untracked -> (add) Staged -> (commit) Unmodified -> (edit) Modified -> (add) Staged ...

Q1.10 ⭐ What is the difference between HEAD, the working tree and the index?
- Working tree: the actual files on disk that you edit.
- Index (staging area): a snapshot of what the next commit will contain (.git/index).
- HEAD: a pointer to the commit you currently have checked out, usually through a branch.
- `git diff`          = working tree vs index
- `git diff --staged` = index vs HEAD
- `git diff HEAD`     = working tree vs HEAD

Q1.11 What is the .git directory?
- The repository itself. Delete it and the history is gone. Key contents:
  - objects/      all blobs, trees, commits, tags (loose files + packfiles)
  - refs/heads/   local branches (each file holds a commit hash)
  - refs/tags/    tags
  - refs/remotes/ remote-tracking branches
  - HEAD          current branch (e.g. "ref: refs/heads/main")
  - index         the staging area
  - config        repo-level configuration
  - hooks/        client-side hook scripts
  - logs/         the reflog

Q1.12 How do you configure Git? What are the configuration levels?
- `git config --system` (all users), `--global` (your user, ~/.gitconfig), `--local` (this repo, .git/config).
- More specific levels override broader ones: local > global > system.
- First-time setup: `git config --global user.name "..."` and `git config --global user.email "..."`.

Q1.13 How can you create an alias in Git?
- `git config --global alias.st status`
- `git config --global alias.lg "log --oneline --graph --decorate --all"`
- After that, `git st` and `git lg` work.


==================================================================================================
2. CORE WORKFLOW & EVERYDAY COMMANDS
==================================================================================================

Q2.1 How will you create a Git repository?
- New project: `git init` (creates .git in the current folder, with default branch main/master).
- Existing remote project: `git clone <url>`.
- Then connect a new repo to a remote: `git remote add origin <url>` and `git push -u origin main`.

Q2.2 ⭐ What is the difference between git init and git clone?
- `git init` creates a new, empty repo locally. No history, no remote.
- `git clone <url>` copies an existing repo with its full history, creates remote-tracking branches,
  sets `origin`, and checks out the default branch.

Q2.3 What does git clone do? (step by step)
1. Creates the folder and runs `git init`.
2. Adds the remote `origin` pointing to the URL.
3. Fetches all objects and branches (as origin/*).
4. Creates a local branch for the default branch that tracks origin/<default>, and checks it out.
- Useful options: `--depth 1` (shallow), `--branch <name>`, `--filter=blob:none` (partial clone),
  `--recurse-submodules`.

Q2.4 ⭐ What is git add?
- Moves changes from the working tree into the staging area.
- `git add file`, `git add .` (current directory), `git add -A` (everything, including deletions).
- ⭐ `git add -p` stages part of a file, hunk by hunk. It is useful for making small, focused commits.

Q2.5 ⭐ What is git status?
- Shows the current branch, whether it is ahead of or behind its upstream, what is staged, what is
  modified but not staged, and what is untracked.
- `git status -s` gives short output.

Q2.6 ⭐ What is a commit in Git?
- A snapshot of the whole project (not a diff) at a point in time. It stores:
  a tree (the snapshot), parent commit(s), author, committer, timestamp and message.
- It is identified by a SHA-1 hash of its content. Change anything and the hash changes, so commits
  are immutable.
- `git commit -m "msg"`, `git commit -am "msg"` (stages tracked files automatically, not new files).

Q2.7 What is the difference between the author and the committer of a commit?
- Author: the person who wrote the change.
- Committer: the person who last applied it (for example through a rebase, cherry-pick or amend).

Q2.8 ⭐ How do you change the last commit in Git?
- `git commit --amend -m "new message"` changes the message.
- Forgot a file: `git add file` then `git commit --amend --no-edit`.
- Amend creates a NEW commit with a new hash. If the old one was already pushed, you need
  `git push --force-with-lease`, so only do this on your own branch.

Q2.9 What is the git restore command? (Git 2.23+)
- `git restore <file>` discards working-tree changes (brings back the index version).
- `git restore --staged <file>` unstages a file (the old way: `git reset HEAD <file>`).
- `git restore --source=<commit> <file>` restores a file from any commit.
- It exists to split the overloaded `git checkout` into clearer commands.

Q2.10 What is git checkout? How do you switch branches in Git?
- `checkout` is an older, overloaded command: it switches branches, restores files and detaches HEAD.
- Modern replacements:
  - `git switch <branch>`, `git switch -c <new-branch>`, `git switch -` (go to the previous branch)
  - `git restore <file>`
- Old style: `git checkout <branch>`, `git checkout -b <new>`, `git checkout -- <file>`.

Q2.11 What is the purpose of the git clean command?
- Removes UNTRACKED files from the working tree. This cannot be undone, because Git never tracked them.
- `git clean -n` dry run (always run this first), `-f` force, `-d` include directories,
  `-x` also remove ignored files (for example build output), `-i` interactive.

Q2.12 How does Git handle file deletion?
- Deleting a file with `rm`: Git shows it as "deleted", unstaged. You still need `git add`/`git rm`.
- `git rm file`: deletes it from disk and stages the deletion.
- ⭐ `git rm --cached file`: stops tracking the file but KEEPS it on disk. Use this for files you
  committed by mistake and now want to ignore.
- The file still exists in older commits. Deleting it does not remove it from history.

Q2.13 How does Git handle renames?
- Git does not record renames. It stores snapshots, and `git log --follow` / `git diff -M` DETECT
  renames by comparing content similarity.
- `git mv old new` is just a shortcut for mv + rm + add.

Q2.14 ⭐ What is the purpose of the .gitignore file?
- Lists patterns of files Git should not track (build output, node_modules, .env, IDE files, logs).
- Patterns: `*.log`, `build/`, `!keep.log` (exception), `/root-only.txt`, `**/temp`.
- It only affects UNTRACKED files. A file that is already tracked keeps being tracked, so you need
  `git rm --cached` first.
- Other options: `.git/info/exclude` (local-only ignore) and a global ignore file
  (`core.excludesFile`) for OS/IDE files.
- Check why a file is ignored: `git check-ignore -v <file>`.

Q2.15 What does git ls-files do?
- Lists the files in the index (the tracked files).
- `-m` modified, `-d` deleted, `-o` untracked ("others"), `-o -i --exclude-standard` ignored files,
  `-s` shows mode + blob hash + stage number (stages 1/2/3 show up during a merge conflict).

Q2.16 How do you view history?
- `git log --oneline --graph --decorate --all` shows a visual branch graph.
- `git log -p <file>` shows the patches for a file. `git log --follow <file>` follows it across renames.
- `git log --author="name"`, `--since="2 weeks ago"`, `--grep="JIRA-123"`.
- ⭐ `git log -S "functionName"` ("pickaxe") finds commits that added or removed that string.
- `git log -L 10,20:file` shows the history of specific lines.
- `git show <commit>` shows one commit. `git shortlog -sn` shows commit counts per author.

Q2.17 Why doesn't Git track empty directories?
- Git tracks files (blobs), not folders. Trees only exist when they contain something.
- Convention: add a placeholder file such as `.gitkeep`.


==================================================================================================
3. REMOTES & COLLABORATION
==================================================================================================

Q3.1 ⭐ What is origin in Git?
- The default NAME given to the remote you cloned from. It is just an alias for a URL. There is
  nothing special about it and it can be renamed.
- `upstream` is the conventional name for the ORIGINAL repo when you work on a fork.

Q3.2 What is the difference between git remote and git clone?
- `git clone` creates a new local copy of a remote repo, and configures origin automatically.
- `git remote` manages remote connections in an EXISTING repo:
  `git remote -v`, `git remote add <name> <url>`, `git remote set-url`, `git remote remove`,
  `git remote rename`.

Q3.3 ⭐ What is the git push command?
- Uploads your local commits to a remote branch: `git push origin feature`.
- `git push -u origin feature` sets upstream tracking, so later a plain `git push`/`git pull` works.
- A push is rejected if the remote has commits you don't have (non-fast-forward). Fetch and integrate first.
- `git push origin --delete feature` deletes a remote branch. `git push --tags` pushes tags.

Q3.4 ⭐ --force vs --force-with-lease
- `--force` overwrites the remote branch no matter what. It can silently wipe out a teammate's pushed commits.
- `--force-with-lease` only overwrites if the remote is still where you last saw it. If someone
  pushed in between, it refuses. Always prefer this.
- Never force-push to shared branches like main/develop. Protect them with branch protection rules.

Q3.5 ⭐ What is the git pull command?
- `git pull` = `git fetch` + `git merge origin/<branch>` (by default).
- `git pull --rebase` = fetch + rebase your local commits on top. This gives a linear history with no
  "Merge branch 'main' of ..." noise commits.
- Configure it once: `git config --global pull.rebase true` (or `pull.ff only` to only allow fast-forwards).

Q3.6 ⭐ What is the difference between git fetch and git pull?
- `fetch` downloads new commits and updates remote-tracking branches (origin/main), but does NOT touch
  your working tree or local branches. It is always safe.
- `pull` fetches AND integrates into your current branch, which can create merge commits or conflicts.
- Good habit: `git fetch` then `git log HEAD..origin/main` to review what's new, then merge or rebase.

Q3.7 How do you fetch all remote branches?
- `git fetch --all` fetches from all remotes. `git fetch --all --prune` also removes stale
  remote-tracking refs for branches deleted on the remote.
- `git branch -r` lists remote branches. `git branch -a` lists all branches.
- To work on one: `git switch <branch>`. This auto-creates a local branch tracking origin/<branch>.

Q3.8 What are remote-tracking branches and upstream branches?
- Remote-tracking branch (origin/main): your read-only local copy of where the remote branch was at the
  last fetch.
- Upstream: the remote branch your local branch is linked to. It is used for ahead/behind counts and
  for plain push/pull. See it with `git branch -vv`. Set it with `git branch -u origin/feature`.

Q3.9 ⭐ What is the difference between a fork and a clone?
- Fork: a server-side copy of someone else's repo under YOUR GitHub account. It is a GitHub concept,
  not a Git one. It is used when you have no write access (open source).
- Clone: a local copy of any repo on your machine. It is a Git concept.
- Fork workflow: fork -> clone your fork -> `git remote add upstream <original>` -> branch -> push to
  your fork -> open a PR to upstream -> keep in sync with `git fetch upstream` + `git rebase upstream/main`.

Q3.10 ⭐ What are the benefits of using a pull request in a project?
- Code review before merge (quality, knowledge sharing, catching bugs).
- CI runs automatically (tests, lint, security scans) before merging.
- Discussion and an audit trail tied to the change. Links to issues/tickets.
- Enforces policies: required reviewers, CODEOWNERS, status checks.
- Keeps main always deployable.

Q3.11 What is a Git bundle?
- A single file containing Git objects and refs: `git bundle create repo.bundle --all`.
- Used to move a repo or commits WITHOUT network access (air-gapped systems, sending over email/USB).
- You can clone or fetch from it: `git clone repo.bundle myrepo`. Verify with `git bundle verify`.


==================================================================================================
4. BRANCHING & MERGING
==================================================================================================

Q4.1 ⭐ What is a branch in Git? Why is branching cheap in Git?
- A branch is just a movable POINTER (a 41-byte file in refs/heads/) to a commit.
- Creating a branch only writes one small file. Nothing is copied. That's why Git encourages
  branching for every feature or bug fix.
- `git branch` (list), `git branch <name>` (create), `git branch -d` (delete if merged),
  `git branch -D` (force delete), `git branch -m old new` (rename).

Q4.2 ⭐ What is HEAD in Git?
- A pointer to your current position. Usually it points to a branch (.git/HEAD = "ref: refs/heads/main"),
  and the branch points to a commit.
- When you commit, the branch moves forward and HEAD moves with it.

Q4.3 ⭐ What is a detached HEAD?
- HEAD points directly to a commit instead of a branch (for example after `git checkout <hash>` or a tag).
- You can look around and even commit, but those commits belong to no branch. If you switch away,
  they are only reachable through the reflog and are eventually garbage-collected.
- Fix: `git switch -c new-branch` to keep the work.

Q4.4 What do HEAD~1, HEAD^ and HEAD^2 mean?
- `HEAD~n`: go n generations back following the FIRST parent. `HEAD~1` = the parent of HEAD.
- `HEAD^`  = `HEAD^1` = first parent. `HEAD^2` = SECOND parent (only exists for merge commits).
- `HEAD~2` = grandparent (`HEAD^^`).

Q4.5 ⭐ What is a fast-forward merge vs a three-way merge?
- Fast-forward: the target branch has no new commits since you branched. Git just moves the pointer
  forward. No merge commit, linear history.
- Three-way merge: both branches have diverged. Git uses the two tips plus their COMMON ANCESTOR
  (merge base) to combine the changes and creates a merge commit with 2 parents.
- `git merge --no-ff feature` forces a merge commit even when a fast-forward is possible. This keeps
  a record that a feature branch existed.
- `git merge --ff-only` refuses to merge unless a fast-forward is possible.
- The default merge strategy is "ort" (Git 2.34+, it replaced "recursive").

Q4.6 ⭐ Explain git rebase and when you use it.
- Rebase REPLAYS your commits one by one on top of another base, creating NEW commits (new hashes).
  `git switch feature && git rebase main`
- Result: linear history, as if you had started the feature from the latest main.
- Use it to: update a feature branch with the latest main, clean up local commits before a PR
  (interactive rebase), and avoid noisy merge commits.
- ⭐ GOLDEN RULE: never rebase commits that others have already pulled (a shared/public branch). It
  rewrites history and breaks everyone else's clones.

Q4.7 ⭐ Explain the difference between git merge and git rebase, and when you would use each.
  | Aspect        | Merge                               | Rebase                              |
  |---------------|-------------------------------------|-------------------------------------|
  | History       | Preserved exactly, non-linear       | Rewritten, linear                   |
  | New commits   | One merge commit                    | Every rebased commit gets a new hash|
  | Safety        | Safe on shared branches             | Only on local/private branches      |
  | Conflicts     | Resolved once                       | May resolve per commit being replayed|
  | Traceability  | Shows when branches were integrated | Cleaner, easier `log` and `bisect`  |
- Use MERGE: integrating into shared branches (feature -> main via PR), and when the true history matters.
- Use REBASE: syncing your own feature branch with main, and cleaning up before opening a PR.
- A common team rule: "rebase locally, merge publicly".

Q4.8 ⭐ What is a conflict in Git?
- It happens when Git can't combine changes automatically: both sides changed the SAME lines of the
  same file, or one side edited a file the other deleted (modify/delete), or both renamed it differently.
- It can happen during merge, rebase, cherry-pick, revert, stash pop and pull.
- Git pauses and marks the conflicts in the file:
    <<<<<<< HEAD
    your version (current branch)
    =======
    their version (incoming branch)
    >>>>>>> feature

Q4.9 ⭐ How do you resolve a conflict in Git? (step by step)
1. `git status` shows the conflicted files ("both modified").
2. Open each file and decide: keep yours, keep theirs, or combine them. Remove all markers.
   - Take one whole side: `git checkout --ours file` / `git checkout --theirs file`.
   - ⚠ During a REBASE, "ours" = the branch you're rebasing ONTO (main) and "theirs" = your commits.
     This is the opposite of a merge.
   - Tools: `git mergetool`, the VS Code merge editor.
3. Build and run the tests. A clean text merge can still be logically broken.
4. `git add <file>` marks it as resolved.
5. Finish: `git commit` (merge), `git rebase --continue` (rebase), `git cherry-pick --continue`.
- Get out: `git merge --abort` / `git rebase --abort` returns to the state before the operation.
- Prevention: small PRs, pull or rebase often, short-lived branches, clear code ownership.
- `git config --global merge.conflictstyle zdiff3` also shows the BASE version, which makes
  resolution much easier.

Q4.10 What is a squash merge? Compare PR merge options on GitHub.
- Merge commit: keeps all feature commits plus a merge commit. Full history, but noisy.
- Squash and merge: all PR commits become ONE commit on main. Clean history, one commit per feature,
  easy to revert. The individual commit detail is lost.
- Rebase and merge: replays each commit onto main linearly. No merge commit.

Q4.11 What is `A..B` vs `A...B`?
- `git log main..feature`: commits in feature that are not in main (what the PR would add).
- `git diff main...feature`: diff of feature against the merge base (exactly what a PR shows).


==================================================================================================
5. UNDOING CHANGES   ⭐ (this area separates average from strong candidates)
==================================================================================================

Q5.1 ⭐ Undo map: which command for which situation?
  | Situation                                    | Command                                  |
  |----------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|
  | Discard unstaged changes in a file           | git restore <file>                       |
  | Unstage a file (keep changes)                | git restore --staged <file>              |
  | Fix the last commit (not pushed)             | git commit --amend                       |
  | Undo last commit, keep changes staged        | git reset --soft HEAD~1                  |
  | Undo last commit, keep changes unstaged      | git reset HEAD~1  (--mixed, the default) |
  | Undo last commit and DISCARD changes         | git reset --hard HEAD~1                  |
  | Undo a commit that's already pushed/shared   | git revert <commit>                      |
  | Recover "lost" commits                       | git reflog + git reset / git branch      |
  | Remove untracked files                       | git clean -fd                            |

Q5.2 ⭐ Explain git reset --soft / --mixed / --hard.
- All three move the current BRANCH pointer (and HEAD) to the given commit. They differ in what
  happens to the index and the working tree:
  | Mode     | Branch/HEAD | Index (staging) | Working tree |
  |----------|-------------|-----------------|--------------|
  | --soft   | moved       | untouched       | untouched    |  -> changes appear staged
  | --mixed  | moved       | reset           | untouched    |  -> changes appear unstaged (default)
  | --hard   | moved       | reset           | reset        |  -> uncommitted changes are LOST
- `--hard` destroys uncommitted work permanently. Committed work can still be recovered via the reflog.

Q5.3 ⭐ Explain the difference between reverting and resetting.
- `git reset` MOVES the branch pointer back and effectively erases commits from the branch.
  It rewrites history, so it's fine for local/unpushed commits only.
- `git revert <commit>` creates a NEW commit that applies the inverse of that commit. History is
  preserved and nothing is rewritten, so it's safe for shared/pushed branches.
- Rule: not pushed -> reset is fine. Pushed/shared -> revert.

Q5.4 ⭐ How do you revert a commit that has already been pushed and made public?
- `git revert <hash>` then `git push`. Don't reset + force-push on a shared branch.
- Multiple commits: `git revert <oldest>^..<newest>`, or `git revert --no-commit A B C` then one commit.
- A MERGE commit: `git revert -m 1 <merge-hash>` (-m 1 = keep the mainline, the first parent, and
  undo what the merged branch brought in).
- ⚠ After reverting a merge, merging the same branch again will NOT bring those changes back, because
  Git thinks they're already merged. You have to "revert the revert" first.

Q5.5 ⭐ What is the difference between git reflog and git log?
- `git log` shows the commit ancestry (parents) reachable from the current branch. It is part of the
  repo and is shared when you push.
- `git reflog` is a LOCAL-only journal of every place HEAD (and each branch) has pointed: commits,
  checkouts, resets, rebases, amends. It is not pushed or shared.
- The reflog is the safety net. It lets you find commits "lost" by reset --hard, a bad rebase or a
  deleted branch. Entries expire (by default 90 days, or 30 days for unreachable commits), then
  `git gc` can delete the objects.

Q5.6 How do you recover a dropped stash?
- `git fsck --no-reflog | grep "dangling commit"` (or `git fsck --unreachable`), inspect the
  candidates with `git show`, then `git stash apply <hash>`.


==================================================================================================
6. REWRITING HISTORY & ADVANCED TOOLS
==================================================================================================

Q6.1 ⭐ What is git stash?
- Temporarily shelves uncommitted changes and gives you a clean working tree, without committing.
- `git stash` / `git stash push -m "wip login"`, `-u` (include untracked), `-p` (partial).
- `git stash list`, `git stash show -p stash@{1}`.
- `git stash pop` (apply + remove), `git stash apply` (apply, keep the stash), `git stash drop`, `git stash clear`.
- `git stash branch new-branch`: creates a branch from where the stash was made and applies it there.
  Use this when popping conflicts.

Q6.2 ⭐ What is the purpose of git cherry-pick?
- Copies a specific commit from another branch onto your current branch, as a NEW commit (new hash).
- `git cherry-pick <hash>`, a range `A^..B`, `-x` (adds "cherry picked from ..." to the message,
  which is good for traceability), `-n` (apply without committing).
- Use cases: backport a bug fix to a release branch, rescue one commit from an abandoned branch.
- Risks: duplicate commits with different hashes (which can conflict later), and missing dependent
  commits. Don't use it as a substitute for proper merging.

Q6.3 ⭐ What is interactive rebase (git rebase -i)?
- `git rebase -i HEAD~5` (or `-i main`) opens an editor listing the commits. For each one you choose:
  pick (keep), reword (edit the message), edit (stop to amend or split), squash (combine, keep the
  message), fixup (combine, drop the message), drop (delete), and you can reorder lines to reorder commits.
- Use it to clean up a messy branch before opening a PR.
- ⭐ Workflow tip: `git commit --fixup <hash>`, then `git rebase -i --autosquash main`. The fixups
  get squashed into the right commits automatically.

Q6.4 ⭐ What is git bisect?
- A binary search through history to find the commit that introduced a bug. It needs only log2(n)
  steps: about 10 steps for 1000 commits.
    git bisect start
    git bisect bad                 # current commit is broken
    git bisect good v2.3.0         # this one was fine
    # Git checks out a middle commit -> test -> `git bisect good` or `git bisect bad` ... repeat
    git bisect reset               # when done
- Automate it: `git bisect run npm test` (exit code 0 = good, non-zero = bad).
- This is why small, buildable commits matter.

Q6.5 ⭐ What is the purpose of git tag -a? What are tags?
- A tag is a fixed, human-readable pointer to a specific commit. It is typically used for releases (v1.2.0).
  Unlike a branch, it doesn't move.
- Lightweight tag (`git tag v1.0`): just a name pointing at a commit.
- Annotated tag (`git tag -a v1.0 -m "Release 1.0"`): a full Git object with tagger, date and message,
  and it can be signed (-s). Recommended for releases.
- Tags are NOT pushed by default: `git push origin v1.0` or `git push --tags`
  (`git push --follow-tags` pushes only annotated tags).
- Delete: `git tag -d v1.0` and `git push origin --delete v1.0`.

Q6.6 What is git blame?
- Shows, line by line, which commit and author last changed each line.
- `git blame -L 10,30 file`, `-w` (ignore whitespace), `-C` (detect lines moved or copied from other files).
- Ignore bulk-formatting commits with `--ignore-revs-file .git-blame-ignore-revs`.

Q6.7 What is git rerere?
- "Reuse recorded resolution". Git remembers how you resolved a conflict and auto-applies the same
  resolution next time. Very useful during long rebases or repeated merges.
- `git config --global rerere.enabled true`

Q6.8 What is git worktree?
- Lets you check out multiple branches at the same time in separate folders, sharing one .git.
- `git worktree add ../hotfix hotfix-branch`. Handy for reviewing a PR or doing a hotfix without
  stashing your current work.

Q6.9 What are Git hooks?
- Scripts that run automatically on Git events.
  - Client-side: pre-commit (lint, format, secret scan), commit-msg (enforce the message format),
    pre-push (run tests), post-merge/post-checkout.
  - Server-side: pre-receive, update, post-receive (enforce policies, trigger deploys).
- Stored in .git/hooks, which is not version-controlled. Teams share them with tools like Husky,
  the pre-commit framework, or `core.hooksPath`.
- Client hooks can be skipped (`--no-verify`), so real enforcement belongs in CI and branch protection.

Q6.10 What are submodules and subtrees?
- Submodule: embeds another repo at a pinned commit, stored as a reference. Needs
  `git submodule update --init --recursive`. It is easy to forget to update.
- Subtree: copies the other repo's content into a subfolder, so it's simpler for consumers.
- Many product companies avoid both and use a monorepo or package managers instead.

Q6.11 How does Git handle large repos and large files?
- Git LFS: stores large binaries (images, videos, models) outside the repo and keeps small pointer files in Git.
- Shallow clone: `git clone --depth 1` (no history, fast CI).
- Partial clone: `git clone --filter=blob:none` (downloads file contents on demand).
- Sparse checkout: `git sparse-checkout set services/payments` (check out only part of a monorepo).
- Maintenance: `git gc`, `git maintenance start`.

Q6.12 What is .gitattributes?
- Per-path settings: line-ending normalization (`* text=auto`, `*.sh text eol=lf`), marking binaries,
  LFS tracking, custom diff/merge drivers (for example `merge=union`).

Q6.13 How do you sign commits and why?
- Sign with GPG or SSH keys (`git commit -S`, `commit.gpgsign true`). GitHub shows a "Verified" badge.
- It proves the author's identity, because anyone can set any user.email. Some companies require it
  through branch protection.


==================================================================================================
7. GIT INTERNALS   (product companies like to probe these)
==================================================================================================

Q7.1 ⭐ What is the Git object model?
- Git is a content-addressable key-value store. Key = hash of the content, value = the object.
  There are 4 object types:
  - Blob: the file contents only (no filename, no permissions).
  - Tree: a directory listing that maps names + modes to blobs and sub-trees.
  - Commit: points to one root tree, plus parent(s), author, committer and message.
  - Tag (annotated): points to an object, plus tagger and message.
- Identical content = same hash = stored once, which gives automatic deduplication.
- Objects are zlib-compressed in .git/objects, and later packed into packfiles with delta compression.
- Hash: SHA-1 by default. SHA-256 repos are supported (`git init --object-format=sha256`).
- Explore them yourself: `git cat-file -t <hash>` (type), `git cat-file -p <hash>` (content).

Q7.2 ⭐ Does Git store diffs or snapshots?
- Snapshots. Every commit points to a full tree of the project. Unchanged files just point to the
  same existing blobs, so this is cheap.
- Diffs are computed on the fly when you ask. (Packfiles use delta compression, but only as a
  storage optimization.)

Q7.3 What happens internally when you run git add and git commit?
- `git add`: Git hashes the file content, writes a blob to .git/objects, and updates the index
  entry (path -> blob hash).
- `git commit`: builds tree objects from the index, creates a commit object (tree + parent + metadata
  + message), and moves the current branch ref to the new commit hash.

Q7.4 What are refs?
- Human-readable names that point to hashes: branches (refs/heads), remote-tracking branches
  (refs/remotes), tags (refs/tags), and HEAD (a symbolic ref).
- Too many loose refs get packed into .git/packed-refs.

Q7.5 Why is Git fast?
- Almost everything is local (no network), snapshots with content hashing, cheap refs for branches,
  compressed packfiles, and it's written in C.

Q7.6 How does Git ensure data integrity?
- Every object's name is the hash of its content, and each commit hashes its tree and parents. So
  changing any byte anywhere in history changes every hash after it, and tampering or corruption is
  detectable (`git fsck`).

Q7.7 What is garbage collection in Git?
- `git gc` packs loose objects into packfiles and removes unreachable objects that are older than the
  reflog/prune expiry. This is why "lost" commits can be recovered for a while, but not forever.


==================================================================================================
8. TEAM WORKFLOWS, GITHUB & BEST PRACTICES
==================================================================================================

Q8.1 ⭐ Compare branching strategies: Git Flow vs GitHub Flow vs trunk-based development.
- Git Flow: main + develop + feature/* + release/* + hotfix/*.
  - Good for versioned releases and scheduled release cycles (mobile apps, on-prem software).
  - Heavy: long-lived branches mean big merges.
- GitHub Flow: main is always deployable, short-lived feature branches, PR, merge, deploy.
  - Simple and good for continuous deployment (web apps/SaaS).
- Trunk-based: everyone integrates into main at least daily. Branches live for hours or a day.
  Unfinished features are hidden behind FEATURE FLAGS.
  - Used by Google, Meta and many product companies. Needs strong CI and automated tests.
- Be ready to say which you've used, WHY, and what the pain points were.

Q8.2 ⭐ What makes a good commit and a good commit message?
- Atomic: one logical change per commit, and every commit builds and passes tests.
- Message: a short imperative subject (~50 chars), a blank line, then a body explaining WHY (not what).
- Conventional Commits: `feat(auth): add OTP login`, `fix: ...`, `chore:`, `docs:`, `refactor:`.
  These enable automatic changelogs and semantic versioning.
- Reference the ticket: `PROJ-123`.

Q8.3 What is semantic versioning?
- MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH, for example 2.4.1.
  - MAJOR = breaking change, MINOR = new backward-compatible feature, PATCH = bug fix.
- Releases are tagged in Git (`v2.4.1`) and GitHub Releases are built on those tags.

Q8.4 ⭐ What are branch protection rules / rulesets on GitHub?
- Settings that protect important branches (main/release):
  - require a PR before merging, and N approvals
  - require status checks (CI) to pass, and the branch to be up to date
  - require CODEOWNERS review, signed commits, linear history, resolved conversations
  - block force pushes and deletion, and restrict who can push

Q8.5 What is CODEOWNERS?
- The file `.github/CODEOWNERS` maps paths to owners (users or teams), e.g. `/payments/ @org/payments-team`.
- Owners are auto-requested as reviewers, and they can be made required approvers.

Q8.6 ⭐ What makes a good pull request?
- Small and focused (roughly under 400 lines), a clear title and description (what, why, how it was
  tested, screenshots), linked ticket, CI green, self-reviewed first.
- Draft PRs for early feedback. Respond to review comments. Use squash or rebase according to team policy.

Q8.7 What is GitHub Actions? (connects to your CI/CD topic)
- GitHub's built-in CI/CD. Workflows are YAML files in `.github/workflows/`, triggered by events
  (push, pull_request, schedule, workflow_dispatch).
- Workflow -> jobs (run on runners) -> steps (shell commands or reusable actions).
- Secrets are stored in repo/org/environment settings. Environments can require approvals for deploys.

Q8.8 What GitHub security features should you know?
- Secret scanning + push protection (blocks pushes that contain API keys).
- Dependabot (vulnerable dependency alerts and update PRs).
- Code scanning (CodeQL).
- Signed commits, 2FA, fine-grained personal access tokens, SSH keys.

Q8.9 Monorepo vs polyrepo?
- Monorepo: one repo for many services. Atomic cross-service changes, shared tooling, one version of
  dependencies. Needs sparse checkout, CODEOWNERS and smart CI (only build what changed).
- Polyrepo: one repo per service. Clear ownership and independent releases. Harder to change
  things across services, and versions can drift.

Q8.10 How do you handle line-ending issues across Windows/Mac/Linux?
- Add `.gitattributes` with `* text=auto` (the repo-level fix that works for everyone), and optionally
  `core.autocrlf` (true on Windows, input on Mac/Linux).


==================================================================================================
9. SCENARIO-BASED QUESTIONS  ⭐⭐⭐
   How to answer: (1) clarify whether it's pushed/shared or local, (2) give the exact commands,
   (3) mention the risk and how you'd prevent it next time.
==================================================================================================

--- A. Committed / pushed the wrong thing ---

S1 ⭐ You committed to main instead of a feature branch (NOT pushed yet). Fix it.
    git branch feature/login          # new branch points at your commits
    git reset --hard origin/main      # move main back to match the remote
    git switch feature/login
- If only some commits should move: create the branch, reset main, or cherry-pick the ones you need.

S2 ⭐ Same situation, but you already PUSHED to main.
- If main is shared: do NOT rewrite it. `git revert` the commits on main, then create a feature
  branch from the original commits (or cherry-pick them onto a new branch) and open a proper PR.
- Prevention: branch protection on main (PR required, no direct pushes).

S3 ⭐ The last commit message has a typo.
- Not pushed: `git commit --amend -m "correct message"`.
- Pushed to your own feature branch: amend + `git push --force-with-lease`.
- Pushed to a shared branch: usually leave it. Rewriting shared history costs more than a typo.

S4 You forgot to add a file to the last commit.
    git add missed-file.js
    git commit --amend --no-edit

S5 ⭐ You want to undo the last commit but keep the changes.
- `git reset --soft HEAD~1` (changes stay staged) or `git reset HEAD~1` (changes unstaged).
- If it was pushed to a shared branch, use `git revert HEAD` instead.

S6 You staged a file by mistake.
- `git restore --staged file` (older: `git reset HEAD file`).

S7 ⭐ You committed a file that should have been ignored (e.g. .env, node_modules).
    echo ".env" >> .gitignore
    git rm --cached .env              # (-r for folders)
    git commit -m "Stop tracking .env"
- The file is still in HISTORY. If it contained secrets, see S8.

S8 ⭐⭐ You accidentally pushed a secret (API key, password) to GitHub.
1. ROTATE/REVOKE the secret IMMEDIATELY. This is the most important step. Assume it's already
   compromised, because bots scrape public repos within minutes, and forks/clones/caches keep copies.
2. Remove it from history: `git filter-repo --invert-paths --path config/secrets.json`
   or `--replace-text`, or use BFG Repo-Cleaner. Then force-push all branches and tags.
3. Tell the team to re-clone (their old clones still contain it). Contact GitHub support to purge
   cached views/PR refs if needed.
4. Prevention: .gitignore, environment variables / a secret manager, pre-commit secret scanning
   (gitleaks), GitHub push protection.

S9 You committed a huge file (e.g. a 500MB video) and the push is rejected.
- Not pushed yet: `git reset --soft HEAD~1` (or rebase -i to drop it from older commits), remove the
  file, commit again.
- Already in history: `git filter-repo --strip-blobs-bigger-than 50M`, then move large assets to Git LFS.

--- B. Recovering lost work ---

S10 ⭐⭐ You ran `git reset --hard` by mistake and lost commits. Recover them.
    git reflog                        # find the entry before the reset, e.g. HEAD@{2}
    git reset --hard HEAD@{2}         # or: git branch recovered <hash>
- Uncommitted changes lost by --hard are generally NOT recoverable. Staged-but-uncommitted files can
  sometimes be found as dangling blobs with `git fsck --lost-found`.

S11 ⭐ You deleted a branch that hadn't been merged. Recover it.
- `git reflog` (or the hash printed by `git branch -D`), then `git branch feature <hash>`.
- If it still exists on the remote: `git switch feature` recreates it from origin/feature.

S12 You made commits in a detached HEAD state and then switched branches.
- `git reflog` to find the commit, then `git branch rescue <hash>` (or `git switch -c rescue <hash>`).

S13 A rebase went wrong halfway / made a mess.
- During the rebase: `git rebase --abort`.
- After it finished: `git reflog` shows the pre-rebase state (`ORIG_HEAD` also points to it), then
  `git reset --hard ORIG_HEAD` or `git reset --hard HEAD@{n}`.

--- C. Collaboration & syncing ---

S14 ⭐ `git push` is rejected: "Updates were rejected because the remote contains work you do not have."
- Someone else pushed first. Do NOT force-push.
    git pull --rebase                 # or: git fetch && git rebase origin/main
    # resolve any conflicts, then:
    git push

S15 ⭐ Your feature branch is 3 weeks behind main. How do you update it: merge or rebase?
- Private branch (only you work on it): `git fetch && git rebase origin/main`, resolve conflicts,
  `git push --force-with-lease`. Result: clean, linear history.
- Shared branch (others push to it too): `git merge origin/main`. This is safe and doesn't rewrite history.
- Mention: enable rerere for long rebases, and in future sync often to avoid this.

S16 ⭐⭐ Two developers changed the same line and you get a conflict. Walk me through it.
- The step-by-step from Q4.9: identify the files, understand BOTH intents (read the other PR or talk to
  the developer), combine the logic correctly, remove markers, run the tests, `git add`, continue.
- Stress that you'd talk to the other developer if the intent is unclear, and never blindly pick
  "ours" or "theirs".

S17 A teammate force-pushed / rebased a shared branch, and now your local copy has diverged.
- If you have no local work on it: `git fetch && git reset --hard origin/branch`.
- If you have local commits: `git fetch`, then `git rebase --onto origin/branch <old-base> mybranch`
  (or cherry-pick your commits onto the new remote tip).
- Follow-up: agree as a team never to force-push shared branches, and enable branch protection.

S18 You need to update your fork with the latest changes from the original repo.
    git remote add upstream <original-url>      # once
    git fetch upstream
    git switch main && git rebase upstream/main  # or merge
    git push origin main

S19 How do you check out a teammate's PR locally to test it?
- `gh pr checkout 123`, or `git fetch origin pull/123/head:pr-123 && git switch pr-123`.

S20 You need to rename a branch locally and on the remote.
    git branch -m old-name new-name
    git push origin -u new-name
    git push origin --delete old-name

--- D. Release, hotfix & production ---

S21 ⭐⭐ Production is broken, and a release is in progress on develop. How do you hotfix?
- Git Flow style: branch `hotfix/x` from main (or the production tag), fix, PR, merge to main, tag
  `v1.4.1`, deploy. Then merge or cherry-pick the fix into develop/release so it isn't lost.
- Trunk-based: fix on main (or cherry-pick to the release branch), deploy. Or turn off the feature flag.
- Mention: first stop the bleeding (roll back the deployment or revert), then do the proper fix.

S22 ⭐ You merged a PR to main and it broke production. Undo it.
- `git revert -m 1 <merge-commit-hash>` then push (or use the "Revert" button on the GitHub PR).
  This is a fast, safe rollback that doesn't rewrite shared history.
- Later, to re-merge the fixed feature: revert the revert commit first, then merge the new fixes.

S23 ⭐ You need just one bug-fix commit from develop in the release branch.
- `git switch release/2.0 && git cherry-pick -x <hash>`. Resolve conflicts if any. Make sure
  commits it depends on aren't missing.

S24 ⭐⭐ A bug appeared in production, and it wasn't there 200 commits ago. Find the commit that caused it.
- `git bisect start`, `git bisect bad`, `git bisect good <good-tag>`, test each step (about 8 steps
  for 200 commits), or automate it with `git bisect run ./test.sh`. Then `git bisect reset`.
- Alternative clues: `git log -S "suspiciousCode"`, `git log -p -- path/to/file`, `git blame`.

S25 How do you find who changed a particular line, and why?
- `git blame -L 40,50 file` gives the commit, then `git show <hash>` gives the message/PR/ticket.
- `git log -L 40,50:file` shows the full evolution of those lines.

S26 How do you see exactly what changed between two releases?
- `git log --oneline v1.2.0..v1.3.0` (commits), `git diff v1.2.0 v1.3.0 --stat` (files).

--- E. Cleaning up history ---

S27 ⭐ Your PR has 15 messy commits ("wip", "fix typo", "fix again"). Clean it up before merging.
- `git rebase -i origin/main`, mark the commits squash/fixup, reword the messages, then
  `git push --force-with-lease`.
- Or just use GitHub's "Squash and merge".

S28 One big commit should really be three separate commits. Split it.
    git rebase -i HEAD~3              # mark that commit as "edit"
    git reset HEAD~1                  # un-commit it, keep the changes
    git add -p ...; git commit        # repeat for each logical piece
    git rebase --continue

S29 You need to remove or change a commit that is in the MIDDLE of a pushed branch.
- Your own branch: `git rebase -i`, drop/edit it, `--force-with-lease`.
- Shared branch: `git revert <hash>` instead.

S30 Your recent commits have the wrong author email.
- Last commit: `git commit --amend --reset-author --no-edit` (after fixing `user.email`).
- Several commits: `git rebase -i <base> --exec "git commit --amend --reset-author --no-edit"`.
- All history: `git filter-repo --mailmap`. Only before pushing, or with team agreement.

--- F. Working context ---

S31 ⭐ You're in the middle of a feature and an urgent bug comes in. Switch contexts safely.
- `git stash push -u -m "wip feature"` -> `git switch main` -> fix on a new branch -> come back ->
  `git stash pop`.
- Or commit a WIP commit and amend it later.
- Or `git worktree add ../hotfix main` so you don't have to touch your current work at all.

S32 `git stash pop` caused conflicts.
- Resolve them like any conflict, then `git add`. The stash is NOT dropped when pop conflicts, so run
  `git stash drop` after resolving.
- Or `git stash branch temp` to apply it on the commit where it was created.

S33 During a long rebase you keep resolving the SAME conflict repeatedly.
- Enable `git config rerere.enabled true`. Or squash your commits first, or merge instead of rebase.

S34 Cloning the company monorepo takes 30 minutes. How do you speed it up (for yourself and for CI)?
- `git clone --filter=blob:none` (partial), `--depth 1` for CI builds, `git sparse-checkout` for the
  folders you need, Git LFS for binaries, and `git maintenance`.

S35 Code keeps getting pushed without passing lint or tests. How do you enforce quality?
- Locally: pre-commit/pre-push hooks (Husky, pre-commit).
- For real: CI checks on every PR, plus branch protection requiring green checks and reviews.
  Hooks can be bypassed, so CI is the actual gate.

S36 A file shows as changed on every checkout, even though nobody edited it.
- Usually line endings (CRLF vs LF) or file mode changes.
- Fix: `.gitattributes` with `* text=auto`, renormalize with `git add --renormalize .`, or
  `git config core.fileMode false`.

S37 On Mac you renamed `readme.md` to `README.md`, but Git doesn't see the change.
- The macOS filesystem is case-insensitive. Use `git mv readme.md README.md` (or go through a temp
  name: `git mv readme.md tmp && git mv tmp README.md`).

S38 You need to delete all local branches that are already merged.
    git fetch --prune
    git branch --merged main | grep -vE '^\*|main|develop' | xargs git branch -d

S39 (Open question) "Describe a time Git saved you, or a Git mistake you made."
- Prepare a real 1-2 minute story using STAR (Situation, Task, Action, Result). For example: a reflog
  recovery, a bisect hunt, or fixing a bad merge on main. It shows real experience, not memorisation.


