10. GIT FOR SALESFORCE DEVOPS  (your target role)
==================================================================================================

Q10.1 Why is source-driven development important in Salesforce?
- Orgs can be changed by clicking, so the org itself is not a reliable source of truth. With SFDX
  source format, Git becomes the source of truth. Every change is versioned, reviewed through PRs, and
  deployed by CI (sf CLI) instead of change sets.

Q10.2 What is a typical Salesforce branching model?
- feature/* -> develop (Dev/Integration sandbox) -> release/QA (QA sandbox) -> UAT -> main (Production).
- Each branch merge triggers a CI deployment to the matching org. Hotfix branches come from main.

Q10.3 What are common merge-conflict hotspots in Salesforce metadata?
- Profiles, permission sets, layouts, custom labels, .flow-meta.xml, and object XML files
  (many developers edit one big XML file).
- Mitigation: prefer permission sets over profiles, decompose metadata where supported, keep PRs
  small, and sort and format XML consistently.

Q10.4 How do you do delta (incremental) deployments?
- Use `sfdx-git-delta` (sgd) to generate package.xml and destructiveChanges.xml from
  `git diff <from> <to>`, then deploy only what changed. This makes deployments much faster than full deploys.

Q10.5 Scenario: a hotfix went directly to Production (for example in Setup), and the org no longer matches Git.
- Retrieve the changed metadata (`sf project retrieve start`), commit it on a hotfix branch, PR into
  main, then back-merge into develop. Afterwards, restrict direct production changes.

Q10.6 What should never be committed in a Salesforce repo?
- Auth URLs, JWT keys, `.sfdx/` or `.sf/` folders, org credentials. Store them in CI secrets.


